 "stimulus_html": "<p>“Where the money is to come from which will defray this enormous annual expense of <i>three millions</i> sterling, and all those other debts, I know not; unless the author of <i>Common Sense</i>, or some other ingenious projector, can discover the <i>Philosopher’s Stone</i>, by which iron and other base metals may be transmuted into gold. Certain I am that our commerce and agriculture, the two principal sources of our wealth, will not support such an expense. The whole of our exports from the Thirteen United Colonies in the year 1769, amounted only to £2,887,898 sterling; which is not so much, by near half a million, as our annual expense would be, were we Independent of Great-Britain. Those exports, with no inconsiderable part of the profits arising from them, it is well known, centered finally in Britain, to pay the merchants and manufacturers there for goods we had imported thence; and yet left us still in debt! What then must our situation be, or what the state of our trade, when oppressed with such a burden of annual expense! When every article of commerce, every necessary of life, together with our lands, must be heavily taxed, to defray that expense!”</p>\n<p align=\"right\">Charles Inglis, Anglican Church Minister in New York City, <i>The Costs of Revolution</i>, 1776</p>\n<p><i>Refer to the excerpt provided.</i></p>",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 615018,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Which of the following conclusions can be drawn from the excerpt provided?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1168045,
     "content_html": "Economic concerns influenced the decision of some to stay loyal to Great Britain."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168044,
     "content_html": "The primary concerns for many were the Enlightenment ideals of freedom and liberty."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168043,
     "content_html": "Colonists along the Atlantic seaboard agreed that taxes were a necessary part of the economy."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168042,
     "content_html": "Independence was the solution to the financial woes of the merchants in the colonies."
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "multiple-choice"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 615019,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Which of the following places the sentiments in the excerpt in proper historical context?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1168048,
     "content_html": "Colonists traded with all European powers equally, competing for the best profits."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168049,
     "content_html": "A system of mercantilism limited the colonists’ legal trade options and, therefore, their profits."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168047,
     "content_html": "Countries poured money into researching alchemy to create gold out of other minerals."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168046,
     "content_html": "Colonies had been illegally printing paper currency to offset the large amount of taxes levied on them by the British."
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "multiple-choice"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 615020,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Which of the following post-war developments ensured a continuation of the sentiments provided?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1168052,
     "content_html": "U.S. products were well made and able to compete internationally, producing quick profits for the new country."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168053,
     "content_html": "British manufactured goods continued to flood the market and fare better than U.S. products for decades after the conflict."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168051,
     "content_html": "U.S. merchants were able to trade with all countries, allowing them to quickly pay off their debts."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168050,
     "content_html": "British taxes continued to hurt the U.S. economy as the new nation struggled to pay war debts and taxes."
    }
   ],
